foundation.question_text:
Sketch the graph of $y=2^{x}+1$. Label the $y$-intercept, the horizontal asymptote, and the point where $x=1$.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-4.5, xmax=3.2, ymin=-1, ymax=6, samples=120, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,-2,2}, ytick={1,2,3,4,5}]
\addplot[thick, dashed, domain=-4.5:2.55] {exp(x*ln(2))};
\node[above left] at (axis cs:2,4) {$y=2^{x}$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.solution_text:
$y=2^{x}+1$
$y$-intercept: let $x=0$,
$y=2^{0}+1$
$=2$
Asymptote: $y=1$
When $x=1$:
$y=2^{1}+1$
$=3$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\begin{axis}[axis lines=middle, xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, xmin=-4.5, xmax=3.2, ymin=-1, ymax=6, samples=120, clip=false, axis line style={-{Stealth}}, xtick={-4,-2,2}, ytick={1,2,3,4,5}]
\addplot[thick, dashed, domain=-4.5:2.55] {exp(x*ln(2))};
\addplot[thick, blue, <->, domain=-4.5:2.3] {exp(x*ln(2))+1};
\addplot[dashed, red, domain=-4.5:3.2] {1};
\node[below] at (axis cs:-3,1) {$y=1$};
\addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(0,2) (1,3)};
\node[above left] at (axis cs:0,2) {$(0,2)$};
\node[right] at (axis cs:1,3) {$(1,3)$};
\end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

